Large Bowel Obstruction (Colorectal Malignancy)
Hydration, Fluid and Volume abnormalities Gastrointestinal Loss
Progressive abdominal distension, Absolute constipation, Late feculent vomiting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: HFV-024
Reason for Visit: Absolute constipation, massive abdominal swelling, and sudden onset of foul-smelling vomiting requiring immediate evaluation for bowel obstruction and impending perforation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a patient unable to pass flatus for four days, presenting with a massively distended, painful abdomen and recent vomiting of dark, stool-like material. This clinical picture indicated a critical, late-stage mechanical bowel obstruction. Immediate after-hours assessment was mandatory to evaluate for peritonitis, severe third-space fluid loss, and to expedite emergency surgical transfer before fatal bowel ischemia occurred.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Huge swollen stomach, terrible cramping, and vomiting foul fluid. Patient states, My stomach is stretched tight like a drum, I haven't passed any wind in days, and I just threw up something that smelled exactly like feces.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have evolved over several days, characterized by absolute constipation with a complete inability to pass flatus or stool. The patient reports marked and progressive, somewhat asymmetric, abdominal distension. This is accompanied by deep, visceral, crampy lower abdominal pain occurring in intense spasms. This evening, the patient experienced late-onset, feculent vomiting secondary to massive retrograde bowel dilation. The patient feels extremely weak and dizzy. There is a preceding history of unexplained chronic weight loss and mild fatigue (anemia) over the last six months, raising high suspicion for an underlying malignant etiology.
Functional Status: Bedbound, writhing in pain during spasms; completely dependent on spouse for assistance.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate surgical emergency; spouse is highly anxious but facilitating emergency care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 100/65 mmHg | HR: 118 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 72.0 Kg
General: Patient is acutely distressed, pale, diaphoretic, and guarding the massively distended abdomen.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Tachycardia is prominent, indicating severe third-space fluid depletion into the obstructed bowel lumen. Delayed capillary refill (3 seconds). Respiratory: Tachypneic, likely secondary to diaphragmatic splinting from the massive abdominal distension. Gastrointestinal: The diagnostic triad is overtly present: progressive abdominal distension is visually massive, absolute constipation is confirmed historically, and late feculent vomiting is witnessed in the emesis basin. Percussion reveals a loud tympanic sound across all quadrants. Auscultation reveals high-pitched, tinkling bowel sounds with intermittent periods of complete absence. The abdomen is tense, but lacks rigid, board-like peritonitis, suggesting perforation has not yet occurred. A vague palpable lower abdominal mass is suspected in the left lower quadrant, but deep palpation is limited by distension.
Environmental Observations: Foul-smelling emesis present in a bedside container.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes no constipating agents (e.g., opioids, anticholinergics) that would cause a simple pseudo-obstruction.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Large Bowel Obstruction (Colorectal Malignancy) - Status: Acute Surgical Emergency
Differentials:

Severe Constipation/Fecal Impaction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of massive, tense distension and frank feculent vomiting, indicating a high-pressure mechanical blockade rather than simple lower rectal impaction.

Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The highly progressive, massive nature of the distension and the very late onset of vomiting (which occurs early in SBO), alongside the history of weight loss pointing to a colonic primary source.

Gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absolute constipation and massive tympanic swelling, completely lacking the voluminous diarrhea characteristic of infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering from an acute mechanical large bowel obstruction, highly likely secondary to an underlying colorectal malignancy given the prodromal weight loss. The progression to feculent vomiting and significant tachycardia demonstrates massive third-spacing of fluid into the dilated bowel and impending ischemic necrosis of the cecum due to extreme back-pressure. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period carried a near-certain risk of catastrophic bowel perforation and fatal fecal peritonitis. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critical to confirm the diagnosis, strictly withhold all oral intake, rule out generalized peritonitis, and immediately coordinate Priority 1 ambulance transport for life-saving surgical decompression. The expected investigation results include massive peripheral colonic dilation with a transition point and absence of distal rectal gas on computed tomography of the abdomen that would assist in confirming the diagnosis upon admission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Made the patient strictly Nil By Mouth (NBM). Positioned the patient comfortably with knees slightly flexed to reduce abdominal wall tension. Coordinated immediate emergency ambulance transport to the nearest Emergency Department for surgical review.
Positive Behavioral Change: (Directed to patient and spouse): To prevent dangerous aspiration of the stomach contents into the lungs, you must strictly refuse to drink any water, even if intensely thirsty. When you actively resist the urge to drink, recognize that you are directly preventing a fatal lung complication, keeping your airway safe for the surgical team.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate handover to Emergency Department and General Surgical teams.

